-- sensors.rofl — paradigm litmus (Appendix B of START.md).
-- three sources, one lying; bridge decides; triad exercised

reading[s1](t1, 20) @init.
reading[s2](t1, 21) @init.
reading[s3](t1, 95) @init.
authority(s1, sensor_net).  authority(s2, sensor_net).  authority(s3, sensor_net).

-- [trust] corroborates one sensor against ANY other: `corroborated[trust]`
-- reads a second reading under the perspective VARIABLE [S2]. That is a
-- crossing `imports` cannot license, since `$var("S2")` is not a registered
-- perspective, and it is the whole mechanism -- corroboration that named its
-- corroborator in advance would not be corroboration. Declared here, at the
-- ledger that gathers.
collects(trust).

close(V1, V2)          :- D is V1 - V2, D <= 2, D >= -2.  -- mode note: both bound
corroborated[trust](S) :- reading[S](T, V1), reading[S2](T, V2),
                          S != S2, close(V1, V2).
outlier[trust](S)      :- reading[S](T, _), not corroborated[trust](S).
temp[verified](T, V)   :- reading[S](T, V), corroborated[trust](S).

-- Acceptance scenario:
--   ? temp[verified](t1, V)      -> 20, 21 (never 95)
--   why  outlier[trust](s3)      -> finite-failure demo of corroboration
--   excise reading[s1](t1, 20)   -> corroborated(s2) falls, temp loses 21:
--                                  the diff IS the blast radius
--   whynot corroborated[trust](s3) -> names the missing close reading
--                                    (single-step abduction)
--   ? leak[audit](A, B)          -> FOUR rows; see below

-- One walk remains, and it is left firing deliberately:
--
--   main -> trust -> verified
--       `close/2` has no bracket, so it lives in [main], and
--       `corroborated[trust]` reads it, and `temp[verified]` reads [trust].
--       `collects` does not reach this one and should not: [main] IS a
--       registered perspective, so the audit is right to keep asking. It
--       could be silenced with `imports(verified, main)`, and that sentence
--       would be false about this program -- what crosses is an arithmetic
--       helper, not content. This file is the standing example of the open
--       decision in docs/books-and-permission.md 5.1, and the repair belongs
--       there rather than in a declaration written to make a row go away.
--
-- HOW THAT ONE WALK BECAME FOUR ROWS, and none of the four is new damage.
-- `crossing` used to carry `not bridge_decl(R, A, B)`, a row the kernel
-- emitted for any rule whose head named a ledger and whose body read another,
-- so the audit could only ever see a walk of two hops or more -- which is why
-- this one first surfaced as its far end `main -> verified` alone. Removing
-- that self-licence added `main -> trust`, the same walk named where it
-- starts. Moving the kernel's reflection into [$kernel] then added
-- `$kernel -> trust` and `$kernel -> verified`, the same walk named one hop
-- further back again. Measured 2026-09-08 on bare boot.rofl plus this file:
-- leak 4, crossing 8, stable across the last four commits.
--
-- The third row it also surfaced, `trust -> verified`, is NOT that walk and
-- IS declarable, so it is declared. `temp[verified](T, V) :- reading[S](T, V),
-- corroborated[trust](S).` reads the corroboration book on purpose: a verified
-- reading is one that [trust] vouched for, and a rule that could not read
-- [trust] could not say so. This is the crossing the old audit was blind to.
imports(verified, trust).

-- The two `main -> ...` rows are still NOT declared, for the reason written
-- above: the sentence would be false about this program.
